! The `ph.x` input that generated `reference.out.ph-si-epsilon`, so that the
! reference is regenerable rather than merely committed.
!
!   pw.x -in si-epsilon.in && ph.x -in si-epsilon.ph.in
!
! with `outdir` pointing at the same scratch for both. The dielectric constant
! and the Born charges are what `epsil = .true.` prints at the end.
! (norm-conserving silicon.)
dielectric constant of silicon, norm-conserving
 &inputph
  tr2_ph = 1.0d-14,
  epsil = .true.,
  fildyn = 'si.dynG',
  amass(1) = 28.086,
 /
0.0 0.0 0.0
